## iti53
iti53:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti53:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these three feelings. | T: “Monks, there are these three feelings.
iti53:2.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
iti53:2.3 | S: Pleasant, painful, and neutral feeling. | T: A feeling of pleasure, a feeling of pain, a feeling of neither pleasure nor pain.
iti53:2.4 | S: Pleasant feeling should be seen as suffering. | T: A feeling of pleasure should be seen as stressful.
iti53:2.5 | S: Painful feeling should be seen as a dart. | T: A feeling of pain should be seen as an arrow.
iti53:2.6 | S: Neutral feeling should be seen as impermanent. | T: A feeling of neither pleasure nor pain should be seen as inconstant.
iti53:2.7 | S: When a mendicant has seen these three feelings in this way, | T: When a monk has seen a feeling of pleasure as stressful, a feeling of pain as an arrow, and a feeling of neither pleasure nor pain as inconstant,
iti53:2.8 | S: they’re called a noble mendicant who sees rightly, has cut off craving, cast off the fetters, and by rightly comprehending conceit has made an end of suffering.” | T: then he is called a monk who is noble, who has seen rightly, who has cut off craving, destroyed the fetters, and who–from the right breaking-through of conceit–has put an end to suffering & stress.”
iti53:2.9 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti53:2.10 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti53:3.1 | S: “A mendicant who sees pleasure as pain, | T: Whoever sees pleasure as stress,
iti53:3.2 | S: and suffering as a dart, | T: sees pain as an arrow,
iti53:3.3 | S: and that peaceful, neutral feeling | T: sees peaceful neither pleasure nor pain
iti53:3.4 | S: as impermanent | T: as inconstant:
iti53:4.1 | S: sees rightly. | T: he is a monk who’s seen rightly.
iti53:4.2 | S: And when freed in regards to that, | T: From that he is there released.
iti53:4.3 | S: that peaceful sage, with perfect insight, | T: A master of direct knowing, at peace, he is a sage
iti53:4.4 | S: has truly slipped their yokes.” | T: gone beyond bonds.
iti53:5.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti53:5.2 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti63
iti63:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti63:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these three periods. | T: “Monks, there are these three times.
iti63:2.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
iti63:2.3 | S: Past, future, and present. | T: Past time, future time, present time.
iti63:2.4 | S: These are the three periods.” | T: These are the three times.”
iti63:2.5 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti63:2.6 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti63:3.1 | S: “Sentient beings who perceive the communicable, | T: Perceiving in terms of signs, beings
iti63:3.2 | S: become established in the communicable. | T: take a stand on signs.
iti63:3.3 | S: Not understanding the communicable, | T: Not fully comprehending signs, they
iti63:3.4 | S: they fall under the yoke of Death. | T: come into the bonds of death.
iti63:4.1 | S: But having fully understood the communicable, | T: But fully comprehending signs, one
iti63:4.2 | S: they don’t conceive a communicator, | T: doesn’t construe a signifier.
iti63:4.3 | S: as they’ve touched liberation with their mind, | T: Touching liberation with the heart,
iti63:4.4 | S: the supreme state of peace. | T: the state of peace unsurpassed,
iti63:5.1 | S: Accomplished in the communicable, | T: consummate in terms of signs,
iti63:5.2 | S: peaceful, in love with the state of peace; | T: peaceful, delighting in the peaceful state,
iti63:5.3 | S: one who makes use after appraisal, firm in principle, | T: judicious, an attainer-of-wisdom makes use of classifications
iti63:5.4 | S: a knowledge master cannot be reckoned.” | T: but can’t be classified.
iti63:6.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti63:6.2 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn3.21
sn3.21:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Near Sāvatthī.
sn3.21:1.2 | S: Then King Pasenadi of Kosala went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him: | T: Then King Pasenadi Kosala went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, the Blessed One said to him,
sn3.21:1.3 | S: “Great king, these four individuals are found in the world. | T: “Great king, there are these four types of people to be found existing in the world.
sn3.21:1.4 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
sn3.21:1.5 | S: The dark bound for darkness, | T: One in darkness who is headed for darkness,
sn3.21:1.6 | S: the dark bound for light, | T: one in darkness who is headed for light,
sn3.21:1.7 | S: the light bound for darkness, | T: one in light who is headed for darkness,
sn3.21:1.8 | S: and the light bound for light. | T: and one in light who is headed for light.
sn3.21:2.1 | S: And how is an individual dark and bound for darkness? | T: “And how is one the type of person in darkness who is headed for darkness?
sn3.21:2.2 | S: It’s when an individual is reborn in a low family—a family of corpse-workers, bamboo-workers, hunters, chariot-makers, or scavengers—poor, with little to eat or drink, where life is tough, and food and shelter are hard to find. | T: There is the case where a person is born into a lower class family—the family of a scavenger, a hunter, a basket-weaver, a wheelwright, or a sweeper—a family that is poor, with little food or drink, living in hardship, where food & clothing are hard to come by.
sn3.21:2.3 | S: And they’re ugly, unsightly, deformed, sickly—one-eyed, crippled, lame, or half-paralyzed. They don’t get to have food, drink, clothes, and vehicles; garlands, fragrance, and makeup; or bed, house, and lighting. | T: And he is ugly, misshapen, stunted, & sickly: half-blind or deformed or lame or crippled. He doesn’t receive any (gifts of) food, drink, clothing, or vehicles; garlands, perfumes, or ointments; bedding, shelter, or lamps.
sn3.21:2.4 | S: And they do bad things by way of body, speech, and mind. | T: He engages in bodily misconduct, verbal misconduct, & mental misconduct.
sn3.21:2.5 | S: When their body breaks up, after death, they’re reborn in a place of loss, a bad place, the underworld, hell. | T: Having engaged in bodily misconduct, verbal misconduct, & mental misconduct, he—on the break-up of the body, after death—reappears in a plane of deprivation, a bad destination, a lower realm, hell.
sn3.21:3.1 | S: This individual is like someone who goes from darkness to darkness, from blackness to blackness, from bloodstain to bloodstain. | T: “Just as if a person were to go from obscurity to obscurity, from darkness to darkness, from a blood stain to a blood stain:
sn3.21:3.2 | P: Tathūpamāhaṁ, mahārāja, imaṁ puggalaṁ vadāmi. | T: This person, I tell you, is similar to that.
sn3.21:3.3 | S: That’s how an individual is dark and bound for darkness. | T: This is the type of person in darkness who is headed for darkness.
sn3.21:4.1 | S: And how is an individual dark and bound for light? | T: “And how is one the type of person in darkness who is headed for light?
