## iti22
iti22:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti22:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, don’t fear good deeds. | T: “Monks, don’t be afraid of acts of merit.
iti22:2.2 | S: For ‘good deeds’ is a term for happiness, for what is likable, desirable, and agreeable. | T: This is a synonym for what is blissful, desirable, pleasing, endearing, charming–i.e., acts of merit.
iti22:2.3 | S: I recall undergoing for a long time the likable, desirable, and agreeable results of good deeds performed over a long time. | T: I directly know that, having long performed meritorious deeds, I long experienced desirable, pleasing, endearing, charming results.
iti22:2.4 | S: Having developed a mind of love for seven years, for seven eons of the cosmos contracting and expanding I didn’t return to this world again. | T: Having developed a mind of good will for seven years, then for seven eons of contraction & expansion I didn’t return to this world.
iti22:2.5 | S: As the eon contracted I went to the realm of sublime luminosity. | T: Whenever the eon was contracting, I entered the [realm of] Radiance.
iti22:2.6 | S: As it expanded I was reborn in an empty mansion of divinity. | T: Whenever the eon was expanding, I reappeared in an empty Brahma-abode.
iti22:3.1 | S: There I was the Divinity, the Great Divinity, the vanquisher, the unvanquished, the universal seer, the wielder of power. | T: There I was Brahma, the Great Brahma, the Unconquered Conqueror, Total Seer, Wielder of Power.
iti22:3.2 | S: I was Sakka, lord of gods, thirty-six times. | T: Then for thirty-six times I was Sakka, ruler of the gods.
iti22:3.3 | S: Many hundreds of times I was a king, a wheel-turning monarch, a just and principled king. My dominion extended to all four sides, I achieved stability in the country, and I possessed the seven treasures. | T: For many hundreds of times I was a king, a wheel-turning emperor, a righteous king of Dhamma, conqueror of the four corners of the earth, maintaining stable control over the countryside, endowed with the seven treasures –
iti22:3.4 | S: Not to mention regional kingship! | T: to say nothing of the times I was a local king.
iti22:4.1 | S: Then I thought, | T: The thought occurred to me,
iti22:4.2 | S: ‘Of what deed of mine is this the fruit and result, that I am now so mighty and powerful?’ | T: ‘Of what action of mine is this the fruit, of what action the result, that I now have such great power & might?’
iti22:4.3 | S: Then I thought, | T: Then the thought occurred to me,
iti22:4.4 | S: ‘It is the fruit and result of three kinds of deeds: | T: ‘This is the fruit of my three [types of] action, the result of three types of action, that I now have such great power & might:
iti22:4.5 | S: giving, self-control, and restraint.’” | T: i.e., generosity, self-control, & restraint.’”
iti22:4.6 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti22:4.7 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti22:5.1 | S: “One should practice only good deeds, | T: Train in acts of merit
iti22:5.2 | S: whose happy outcome stretches ahead. | T: that yield the foremost profit of bliss–
iti22:5.3 | S: Giving and moral conduct, | T: (none)
iti22:5.4 | S: developing a mind of love: | T: develop generosity, a life in tune, a mind of good will.
iti22:6.1 | S: having developed these | T: Developing these
iti22:6.2 | S: three things yielding happiness, | T: three things that bring about bliss,
iti22:6.3 | S: that astute one is reborn | T: the wise reappear in a world of bliss
iti22:6.4 | S: in a happy, pleasing world.” | T: unalloyed.
iti22:7.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti22:7.2 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti44
iti44:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti44:2.1 | S: “There are, mendicants, these two elements of extinguishment. | T: “Monks, there are these two forms of the Unbinding property.
iti44:2.2 | S: What two? | T: Which two?
iti44:2.3 | S: The element of extinguishment with residue, and the element of extinguishment with no residue. | T: The Unbinding property with fuel remaining, & the Unbinding property with no fuel remaining.
iti44:3.1 | S: And what is the element of extinguishment with residue? | T: “And what is the Unbinding property with fuel remaining?
iti44:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant is a perfected one, with defilements ended, who has completed the spiritual journey, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, achieved their heart’s goal, utterly ended the fetter of continued existence, and is rightly freed through enlightenment. | T: There is the case where a monk is an Arahant whose effluents have ended, who has reached fulfillment, finished the task, laid down the burden, attained the true goal, destroyed the fetter of becoming, and is released through right gnosis.
iti44:3.3 | S: Their five sense faculties still remain. So long as their senses have not gone they continue to experience the agreeable and disagreeable, to feel pleasure and pain. | T: His five sense faculties still remain and, owing to their being intact, he experiences the pleasing & the displeasing, and is sensitive to pleasure & pain.
iti44:3.4 | S: The ending of greed, hate, and delusion in them | T: His ending of passion, aversion, & delusion
iti44:3.5 | S: is called the element of extinguishment with residue. | T: is termed the Unbinding property with fuel remaining.
iti44:4.1 | S: And what is the element of extinguishment with no residue? | T: “And what is the Unbinding property with no fuel remaining?
iti44:4.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant is a perfected one, with defilements ended, who has completed the spiritual journey, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, achieved their heart’s goal, utterly ended the fetter of continued existence, and is rightly freed through enlightenment. | T: There is the case where a monk is an Arahant whose effluents have ended, who has reached fulfillment, finished the task, laid down the burden, attained the true goal, destroyed the fetter of becoming, and is released through right gnosis.
iti44:4.3 | S: For them, everything that’s felt, being no longer relished, will become cool right here. | T: For him, all that is sensed, being unrelished, will grow cold right here.
iti44:4.4 | S: This is called the element of extinguishment with no residue. | T: This is termed the Unbinding property with no fuel remaining.”
iti44:4.5 | S: These are the two elements of extinguishment.” | T: (none)
iti44:4.6 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti44:4.7 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti44:5.1 | S: “These two elements of extinguishment <j>have been revealed | T: These two proclaimed by the one with vision, Unbinding properties
iti44:5.2 | S: by the Clear-eyed One, <j>the unattached, the unaffected. | T: the one independent, the one who is Such:
iti44:5.3 | S: One element pertains to this life— | T: one property, here in this life,
iti44:5.4 | S: that with residue <j>though the leash to existence has ended; | T: with fuel remaining from the destruction of [craving], the guide to becoming,
iti44:5.5 | S: and that with no residue, <j>which pertains to what follows this life, | T: and that with no fuel remaining, after this life,
iti44:5.6 | S: where all states of existence cease. | T: in which all becoming totally ceases.
iti44:6.1 | S: Those who have fully understood <j>the unconditioned state— | T: Those who know this unfabricated state,
iti44:6.2 | S: their minds freed, the leash to existence ended— | T: their minds released through the destruction of [craving], the guide to becoming,
iti44:6.3 | S: attained to the heart of the Dhamma, <j>they delight in ending, | T: they, attaining the Dhamma’s heartwood, delighting in ending,
iti44:6.4 | S: the unaffected ones have given up <j>all states of existence.” | T: have abandoned all becoming: they, the Such.
iti44:7.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti44:7.2 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## snp5.18
snp5.18:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha while staying in the land of the Magadhans at the Pāsāṇaka Shrine. | T: Epilogue That is what the Blessed One said when dwelling among the Magadhans at the Pāsāṇaka shrine.
snp5.18:1.2 | S: When requested by the sixteen brahmin devotees, he answered their questions one by one. | T: Asked in turn by the sixteen brahmans, he answered their questions.
snp5.18:1.3 | S: If you understand the meaning and the teaching of each of these questions, and practice in line with the teaching, you may go right to the far shore of old age and death. | T: And if one were to practice the Dhamma in accordance with the Dhamma, knowing the meaning and Dhamma of each of these questions, one would go to the far shore of birth & death.
snp5.18:1.4 | S: These teachings are said to lead to the far shore, which is why the name of this exposition of the teaching is “The Way to the Far Shore”. | T: Because these Dhammas lead there, this Dhamma-sequence is called “To the Far Shore.”
snp5.18:2.1 | S: Ajita, Tissametteyya, | T: Ajita, Tissa-metteyya,
snp5.18:2.2 | S: Puṇṇaka and Mettagū, | T: Puṇṇaka & Mettagū,
snp5.18:2.3 | S: Dhotaka and Upasiva, | T: Dhotaka & Upasīva,
snp5.18:2.4 | S: Nanda and then Hemaka, | T: Nanda & Hemaka,
snp5.18:3.1 | S: both Todeyya and Kappa, | T: Todeyya & Kappa,
snp5.18:3.2 | S: and Jatukaṇṇī the astute, | T: the wise Jatukaṇṇin,
snp5.18:3.3 | S: Bhadrāvudha and Udaya, | T: Bhadrāvudha & Udaya,
snp5.18:3.4 | S: and the brahmin Posāla, | T: Posāla the brahman,
snp5.18:3.5 | S: Mogharāja the intelligent, | T: Mogharāja the intelligent,
snp5.18:3.6 | S: and Piṅgiya the great seer: | T: and Piṅgiya the great seer:
snp5.18:4.1 | S: they approached the Buddha, | T: They went to the Awakened One,
snp5.18:4.2 | S: the seer of consummate conduct. | T: consummate in conduct, the seer.
snp5.18:4.3 | S: Asking their subtle questions, | T: They went to the excellent Awakened One, asking subtle questions.
snp5.18:4.4 | S: they came to the most excellent Buddha. | T: (none)
snp5.18:5.1 | S: The Buddha answered their questions | T: The Awakened One, when asked their questions, answered in line with the truth.
snp5.18:5.2 | S: in accordance with truth. | T: (none)
snp5.18:5.3 | S: The sage satisfied the brahmins | T: By answering their questions, the sage delighted the brahmans.
snp5.18:5.4 | S: with his answers to their questions. | T: (none)
snp5.18:6.1 | S: Those who were satisfied by the Clear-eyed One, | T: They, delighted by the One with Eyes—
snp5.18:6.2 | S: the Buddha, Kinsman of the Sun, | T: Awakened, Kinsman of the Sun—
snp5.18:6.3 | S: led the spiritual life in his presence, | T: lived the holy life in the presence of the one
snp5.18:6.4 | S: the one of such outstanding wisdom. | T: of foremost discernment.
snp5.18:7.1 | S: If you practice in accordance | T: Whoever would practice
snp5.18:7.2 | S: with each of these questions | T: as the Awakened One taught concerning each of these questions,
snp5.18:7.3 | S: as taught by the Buddha, | T: (none)
snp5.18:7.4 | S: you’ll go from the near shore to the far. | T: would go from the near shore to the far—
snp5.18:8.1 | S: Developing the supreme path, | T: would go from the near shore to the far developing the path supreme.
snp5.18:8.2 | S: you’ll go from the near shore to the far. | T: (none)
snp5.18:8.3 | S: This path is for going to the far shore; | T: The path is for going beyond,
snp5.18:8.4 | S: that’s why it’s called “The Way to the Far Shore”. | T: and so it’s called “To the Far Shore.”

## an10.18
an10.18:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: (none)
an10.18:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: (none)
an10.18:1.3 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: (none)
an10.18:1.4 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: (none)
an10.18:1.5 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: (none)
an10.18:1.6 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: (none)
an10.18:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, you should live with a protector, not without one. | T: “Live with a protector, monks, and not without a protector.
an10.18:2.2 | S: Living without a protector is suffering. | T: He suffers, one who lives without a protector.
an10.18:2.3 | S: There are ten qualities that serve as protector. | T: And these ten are qualities creating a protector.
an10.18:2.4 | S: What ten? | T: Which ten?
an10.18:2.5 | S: Firstly, a mendicant is ethical, restrained in the monastic code, conducting themselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, they keep the rules they’ve undertaken. | T: “There is the case where a monk is virtuous. He dwells restrained in accordance with the Pāṭimokkha, consummate in his behavior & sphere of activity. He trains himself, having undertaken the training rules, seeing danger in the slightest faults.
an10.18:2.6 | S: Knowing this, the mendicants—whether senior, middle, or junior—think that mendicant is worth advising and instructing. | T: [When they think,] ‘How virtuous is this monk! He dwells restrained in accordance with the Pāṭimokkha, consummate in his behavior & sphere of activity. He trains himself, having undertaken the training rules, seeing danger in the slightest faults,’ elder monks think that he should be corrected & instructed. Even monks of middle standing & newcoming monks think that he should be corrected & instructed.
an10.18:2.7 | S: Being treated with such kindness by the senior, middle, and junior mendicants, that mendicant can expect only growth, not decline. | T: For him—treated with sympathy by the elders, sympathy by those of middle standing, & sympathy by newcomers—growth in skillful qualities is to be expected, and not decline.
an10.18:2.8 | S: This is a quality that serves as protector. | T: This is a quality creating a protector.
an10.18:3.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant is very learned, remembering and keeping what they’ve learned. These teachings are good in the beginning, good in the middle, and good in the end, meaningful and well-phrased, describing a spiritual practice that’s entirely full and pure. They are very learned in such teachings, remembering them, reinforcing them by recitation, mentally scrutinizing them, and penetrating them theoretically. | T: “Then again, the monk has heard much, has retained what he has heard, has stored what he has heard. Whatever teachings are admirable in the beginning, admirable in the middle, admirable in the end, that—in their meaning & expression—proclaim the holy life that is entirely perfect, surpassingly pure: Those he has listened to often, retained, discussed, accumulated, examined with his mind, & well-penetrated in terms of his views.
an10.18:3.2 | S: Knowing this, the mendicants—whether senior, middle, or junior—think that mendicant is worth advising and instructing. | T: [When they think,] ‘How much this monk has heard! Whatever teachings are admirable in the beginning, admirable in the middle, admirable in the end, that—in their meaning & expression—proclaim the holy life that is entirely perfect, surpassingly pure: Those he has listened to often, retained, discussed, accumulated, examined with his mind, & well-penetrated in terms of his views,’ elder monks think that he should be corrected & instructed. Even monks of middle standing & newcoming monks think that he should be corrected & instructed.
an10.18:3.3 | S: Being treated with such kindness by the senior, middle, and junior mendicants, that mendicant can expect only growth, not decline. | T: For him—treated with sympathy by the elders, sympathy by those of middle standing, & sympathy by newcomers—growth in skillful qualities is to be expected, and not decline.
an10.18:3.4 | S: This too is a quality that serves as protector. | T: This, too, is a quality creating a protector.
an10.18:4.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant has good friends, companions, and associates. | T: “Then again, the monk has admirable friends, admirable comrades, admirable companions.
an10.18:4.2 | S: Knowing this, the mendicants—whether senior, middle, or junior—think that mendicant is worth advising and instructing. | T: [When they think,] ‘How this monk has admirable friends, admirable comrades, admirable companions!’ elder monks think that he should be corrected & instructed. Even monks of middle standing & newcoming monks think that he should be corrected & instructed.
an10.18:4.3 | S: Being treated with such kindness by the senior, middle, and junior mendicants, that mendicant can expect only growth, not decline. | T: For him—treated with sympathy by the elders, sympathy by those of middle standing, & sympathy by newcomers—growth in skillful qualities is to be expected, and not decline.
an10.18:4.4 | S: This too is a quality that serves as protector. | T: This, too, is a quality creating a protector.
an10.18:5.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant is easy to admonish, having qualities that make them easy to admonish. They’re patient, and take instruction respectfully. | T: “Then again, the monk is easy to speak to, endowed with qualities that make him easy to speak to, patient, respectful to instruction.
an10.18:5.2 | S: Knowing this, the mendicants—whether senior, middle, or junior—think that mendicant is worth advising and instructing. | T: [When they think,] ‘How easy this monk is to speak to! He is endowed with qualities that make him easy to speak to, patient, respectful to instruction,’ elder monks think that he should be corrected & instructed. Even monks of middle standing & newcoming monks think that he should be corrected & instructed.
an10.18:5.3 | S: Being treated with such kindness by the senior, middle, and junior mendicants, that mendicant can expect only growth, not decline. | T: For him—treated with sympathy by the elders, sympathy by those of middle standing, & sympathy by newcomers—growth in skillful qualities is to be expected, and not decline.
an10.18:5.4 | S: This too is a quality that serves as protector. | T: This, too, is a quality creating a protector.
an10.18:6.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant is deft and tireless in a diverse spectrum of duties for their spiritual companions, understanding how to go about things in order to complete and organize the work. | T: “Then again, the monk is adept at the various affairs involving his companions in the holy life; is vigorous, quick-witted in the techniques involved in them, is up to doing them or arranging to get them done.
an10.18:6.2 | S: Knowing this, the mendicants—whether senior, middle, or junior—think that mendicant is worth advising and instructing. | T: [When they think,] ‘How adept this monk is at the various affairs involving his companions in the holy life! He is vigorous, quick-witted in the techniques involved in them, is up to doing them or arranging to get them done,’ elder monks think that he should be corrected & instructed. Even monks of middle standing & newcoming monks think that he should be corrected & instructed.
an10.18:6.3 | S: Being treated with such kindness by the senior, middle, and junior mendicants, that mendicant can expect only growth, not decline. | T: For him—treated with sympathy by the elders, sympathy by those of middle standing, & sympathy by newcomers—growth in skillful qualities is to be expected, and not decline.
an10.18:6.4 | S: This too is a quality that serves as protector. | T: This, too, is a quality creating a protector.
an10.18:7.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant loves the teachings and is a delight to converse with, being full of joy in the teaching and training. | T: “Then again, the monk is one who desires the Dhamma, endearing in his conversation, greatly rejoicing in the higher Dhamma & higher Vinaya.
an10.18:7.2 | S: Knowing this, the mendicants—whether senior, middle, or junior—think that mendicant is worth advising and instructing. | T: [When they think,] ‘How this monk is one who desires the Dhamma! He is endearing in his conversation, greatly rejoicing in the higher Dhamma & higher Vinaya,’ elder monks think that he should be corrected & instructed. Even monks of middle standing & newcoming monks think that he should be corrected & instructed.
an10.18:7.3 | S: Being treated with such kindness by the senior, middle, and junior mendicants, that mendicant can expect only growth, not decline. | T: For him—treated with sympathy by the elders, sympathy by those of middle standing, & sympathy by newcomers—growth in skillful qualities is to be expected, and not decline.
an10.18:7.4 | S: This too is a quality that serves as protector. | T: This, too, is a quality creating a protector.
an10.18:8.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant lives with energy roused up for giving up unskillful qualities and embracing skillful qualities. They are strong, staunchly vigorous, not slacking off when it comes to developing skillful qualities. | T: “Then again, the monk keeps his persistence aroused for abandoning unskillful qualities & for taking on skillful qualities. He is steadfast, solid in his effort, not shirking his duties with regard to skillful qualities.
an10.18:8.2 | S: Knowing this, the mendicants—whether senior, middle, or junior—think that mendicant is worth advising and instructing. | T: [When they think,] ‘How this monk keeps his persistence aroused for abandoning unskillful qualities & for taking on skillful qualities! He is steadfast, solid in his effort, not shirking his duties with regard to skillful qualities,’ elder monks think that he should be corrected & instructed. Even monks of middle standing & newcoming monks think that he should be corrected & instructed.
an10.18:8.3 | S: Being treated with such kindness by the senior, middle, and junior mendicants, that mendicant can expect only growth, not decline. | T: For him—treated with sympathy by the elders, sympathy by those of middle standing, & sympathy by newcomers—growth in skillful qualities is to be expected, and not decline.
an10.18:8.4 | S: This too is a quality that serves as protector. | T: This, too, is a quality creating a protector.
an10.18:9.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant is content with any kind of robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick. | T: “Then again, the monk is content with any old robe cloth at all, any old alms food, any old lodging, any old medicinal requisites for curing sickness at all.
an10.18:9.2 | S: Knowing this, the mendicants—whether senior, middle, or junior—think that mendicant is worth advising and instructing. | T: [When they think,] ‘How this monk is content with any old robe cloth at all, any old alms food, any old lodging, any old medicinal requisites for curing sickness at all!’ elder monks think that he should be corrected & instructed. Even monks of middle standing & newcoming monks think that he should be corrected & instructed.
an10.18:9.3 | S: Being treated with such kindness by the senior, middle, and junior mendicants, that mendicant can expect only growth, not decline. | T: For him—treated with sympathy by the elders, sympathy by those of middle standing, & sympathy by newcomers—growth in skillful qualities is to be expected, and not decline.
an10.18:9.4 | S: This too is a quality that serves as protector. | T: This, too, is a quality creating a protector.
an10.18:10.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant is mindful. They have utmost mindfulness and alertness, and can remember and recall what was said and done long ago. | T: “Then again, the monk is mindful, endowed with excellent proficiency in mindfulness, remembering & recollecting what was done & said a long time ago.
an10.18:10.2 | S: Knowing this, the mendicants—whether senior, middle, or junior—think that mendicant is worth advising and instructing. | T: [When they think,] ‘How mindful is this monk! He is endowed with excellent proficiency in mindfulness, remembering & recollecting what was done & said a long time ago,’ elder monks think that he should be corrected & instructed. Even monks of middle standing & newcoming monks think that he should be corrected & instructed.
an10.18:10.3 | S: Being treated with such kindness by the senior, middle, and junior mendicants, that mendicant can expect only growth, not decline. | T: For him—treated with sympathy by the elders, sympathy by those of middle standing, & sympathy by newcomers—growth in skillful qualities is to be expected, and not decline.
an10.18:10.4 | S: This too is a quality that serves as protector. | T: This, too, is a quality creating a protector.
an10.18:11.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant is wise. They have the wisdom of arising and passing away which is noble, penetrative, and leads to the complete ending of suffering. | T: “Then again, the monk is discerning, endowed with discernment of arising & passing away—noble, penetrating, leading to the right ending of stress.
an10.18:11.2 | S: Knowing this, the mendicants—whether senior, middle, or junior—think that mendicant is worth advising and instructing. | T: [When they think,] ‘How this monk is discerning! He is endowed with discernment of arising & passing away—noble, penetrating, leading to the right ending of stress,’ elder monks think that he should be corrected & instructed. Even monks of middle standing & newcoming monks think that he should be corrected & instructed.
an10.18:11.3 | S: Being treated with such kindness by the senior, middle, and junior mendicants, that mendicant can expect only growth, not decline. | T: For him—treated with sympathy by the elders, sympathy by those of middle standing, & sympathy by newcomers—growth in skillful qualities is to be expected, and not decline.
an10.18:11.4 | S: This too is a quality that serves as protector. | T: This, too, is a quality creating a protector.
an10.18:12.1 | S: You should live with a protector, not without one. | T: “Live with a protector, monks, and not without a protector.
an10.18:12.2 | S: Living without a protector is suffering. | T: He suffers, one who lives without a protector.
an10.18:12.3 | S: These are the ten qualities that serve as protector.” | T: These are the ten qualities creating a protector.”
an10.18:12.4 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: (none)

