## iti22
iti22:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti22:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, don’t fear good deeds. | T: “Monks, don’t be afraid of acts of merit.
iti22:2.2 | S: For ‘good deeds’ is a term for happiness, for what is likable, desirable, and agreeable. | T: This is a synonym for what is blissful, desirable, pleasing, endearing, charming–i.e., acts of merit.
iti22:2.3 | S: I recall undergoing for a long time the likable, desirable, and agreeable results of good deeds performed over a long time. | T: I directly know that, having long performed meritorious deeds, I long experienced desirable, pleasing, endearing, charming results.
iti22:2.4 | S: Having developed a mind of love for seven years, for seven eons of the cosmos contracting and expanding I didn’t return to this world again. | T: Having developed a mind of good will for seven years, then for seven eons of contraction & expansion I didn’t return to this world.
iti22:2.5 | S: As the eon contracted I went to the realm of sublime luminosity. | T: Whenever the eon was contracting, I entered the [realm of] Radiance.
iti22:2.6 | S: As it expanded I was reborn in an empty mansion of divinity. | T: Whenever the eon was expanding, I reappeared in an empty Brahma-abode.
iti22:3.1 | S: There I was the Divinity, the Great Divinity, the vanquisher, the unvanquished, the universal seer, the wielder of power. | T: There I was Brahma, the Great Brahma, the Unconquered Conqueror, Total Seer, Wielder of Power.
iti22:3.2 | S: I was Sakka, lord of gods, thirty-six times. | T: Then for thirty-six times I was Sakka, ruler of the gods.
iti22:3.3 | S: Many hundreds of times I was a king, a wheel-turning monarch, a just and principled king. My dominion extended to all four sides, I achieved stability in the country, and I possessed the seven treasures. | T: For many hundreds of times I was a king, a wheel-turning emperor, a righteous king of Dhamma, conqueror of the four corners of the earth, maintaining stable control over the countryside, endowed with the seven treasures –
iti22:3.4 | S: Not to mention regional kingship! | T: to say nothing of the times I was a local king.
iti22:4.1 | S: Then I thought, | T: The thought occurred to me,
iti22:4.2 | S: ‘Of what deed of mine is this the fruit and result, that I am now so mighty and powerful?’ | T: ‘Of what action of mine is this the fruit, of what action the result, that I now have such great power & might?’
iti22:4.3 | S: Then I thought, | T: Then the thought occurred to me,
iti22:4.4 | S: ‘It is the fruit and result of three kinds of deeds: | T: ‘This is the fruit of my three [types of] action, the result of three types of action, that I now have such great power & might:
iti22:4.5 | S: giving, self-control, and restraint.’” | T: i.e., generosity, self-control, & restraint.’”
iti22:4.6 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti22:4.7 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti22:5.1 | S: “One should practice only good deeds, | T: Train in acts of merit
iti22:5.2 | S: whose happy outcome stretches ahead. | T: that yield the foremost profit of bliss–
iti22:5.3 | S: Giving and moral conduct, | T: (none)
iti22:5.4 | S: developing a mind of love: | T: develop generosity, a life in tune, a mind of good will.
iti22:6.1 | S: having developed these | T: Developing these
iti22:6.2 | S: three things yielding happiness, | T: three things that bring about bliss,
iti22:6.3 | S: that astute one is reborn | T: the wise reappear in a world of bliss
iti22:6.4 | S: in a happy, pleasing world.” | T: unalloyed.
iti22:7.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti22:7.2 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti44
iti44:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti44:2.1 | S: “There are, mendicants, these two elements of extinguishment. | T: “Monks, there are these two forms of the Unbinding property.
iti44:2.2 | S: What two? | T: Which two?
iti44:2.3 | S: The element of extinguishment with residue, and the element of extinguishment with no residue. | T: The Unbinding property with fuel remaining, & the Unbinding property with no fuel remaining.
iti44:3.1 | S: And what is the element of extinguishment with residue? | T: “And what is the Unbinding property with fuel remaining?
iti44:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant is a perfected one, with defilements ended, who has completed the spiritual journey, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, achieved their heart’s goal, utterly ended the fetter of continued existence, and is rightly freed through enlightenment. | T: There is the case where a monk is an Arahant whose effluents have ended, who has reached fulfillment, finished the task, laid down the burden, attained the true goal, destroyed the fetter of becoming, and is released through right gnosis.
iti44:3.3 | S: Their five sense faculties still remain. So long as their senses have not gone they continue to experience the agreeable and disagreeable, to feel pleasure and pain. | T: His five sense faculties still remain and, owing to their being intact, he experiences the pleasing & the displeasing, and is sensitive to pleasure & pain.
iti44:3.4 | S: The ending of greed, hate, and delusion in them | T: His ending of passion, aversion, & delusion
iti44:3.5 | S: is called the element of extinguishment with residue. | T: is termed the Unbinding property with fuel remaining.
iti44:4.1 | S: And what is the element of extinguishment with no residue? | T: “And what is the Unbinding property with no fuel remaining?

